#--: Overview

The #~"platform" unit is what the compiler knows about the build it
is performing: the operating system and architecture it is building
for, and the C compiler it will hand the emitted C to.

Every definition in it is a #b:"compile-time constant". A read is
folded while compiling, so it can drive a unit-level #~"if" or a
#~"require:" block, and a branch it decides never reaches the C.

#code language=zerolang: """
  require: unit {
      library: if platform.cc == platform.cckind.tcc then {
          error "this unit needs a compiler with __float128"
      } else {
          "quadmath"
      }
  }
"""

Each kind is named for its #b:"values" rather than for the member
that reads it. That is what keeps #~"platform.platform" from ever
arising: the member is #~"os", its type is #~"oskind", and an arm is
#~"platform.oskind.linux".

#p: #b:"Members"

#ul:
  [#*: #> to=os: #b:"platform.os" -- #~"oskind"]
  [#*: #> to=arch: #b:"platform.arch" -- #~"archkind"]
  [#*: #> to=cc: #b:"platform.cc" -- #~"cckind"]
  [#*: #> to=ccmode: #b:"platform.ccmode" -- #~"ccmodekind"]
  [#*: #> to=target: #b:"platform.target" -- #~"String"]

#p: #b:"Kinds"

#ul:
  [#*: #> to=oskind: #b:"oskind" -- #~"variant { linux: null  darwin: null  windows: null  other: null }"]
  [#*: #> to=archkind: #b:"archkind" -- #~"variant { x86_64: null  aarch64: null  other: null }"]
  [#*: #> to=cckind: #b:"cckind" -- #~"variant { gcc: null  clang: null  tcc: null  other: null }"]
  [#*: #> to=ccmodekind: #b:"ccmodekind" -- #~"variant { spawn: null  inproc: null }"]


#--: oskind

#code language=zerolang: """
  oskind: variant {
      linux:   null
      darwin:  null
      windows: null
      other:   null
  }
"""

Host operating-system family.


#--: archkind

#code language=zerolang: """
  archkind: variant {
      x86_64:  null
      aarch64: null
      other:   null
  }
"""

CPU architecture family.


#--: cckind

#code language=zerolang: """
  cckind: variant {
      gcc:   null
      clang: null
      tcc:   null
      other: null
  }
"""

The C compiler #~"zc" will hand the emitted C to, classified by the
#b:"basename" of the resolved path -- the same resolution order
#~"zc" itself uses: #~"--cc", then #~"<target>-gcc", then #~"$CC",
then #~"cc".

Classification is by name alone. Probing #~"--version" would add a
subprocess to every compile, so a #~"--cc" pointing at a wrapper
script reads as #~"other" and a guard keyed on one compiler will not
fire for it.


#--: ccmodekind

#code language=zerolang: """
  ccmodekind: variant {
      spawn:  null
      inproc: null
  }
"""

How #~"zc" reaches that compiler: #~"spawn" runs it as a child
process, #~"inproc" loads it into this one. Selected with
#~"--cc-mode"; the default is #~"spawn".

This is a separate axis from #~"cckind" on purpose, so
#~"platform.cc == platform.cckind.tcc" keeps firing whichever way tcc
is reached. Naming the in-process path as a compiler of its own would
have made every guard already written quietly stop applying to it.

There is no #~"auto", and #~"zc" never falls back: the requested mode
is always the resolved one. That is what keeps this constant
foldable. An #~"auto" would have to probe for a loadable library, and
#~"platform" is read during #b:"type checking" -- so a program would
typecheck differently depending on a machine's shared-library layout,
and the editor would disagree with the build.


#--: os

#code language=zerolang: """
  os: oskind
"""

The operating-system family being built for.

#code language=zerolang: """
  if platform.os.linux then { ... }
  if platform.os == platform.oskind.linux then { ... }
"""

Both spellings fold and agree: #~"value.arm" is always a predicate,
so the first asks whether the constant holds that arm.


#--: arch

#code language=zerolang: """
  arch: archkind
"""

The CPU architecture family being built for.


#--: cc

#code language=zerolang: """
  cc: cckind
"""

The C compiler #~"zc" will invoke.

This is #b:"not" #> to=./stdlib_os.html: #~"os.buildCompiler", which
reports the compiler that built the running #~"zc" binary. This one
is the compiler that #~"zc" is about to run.


#--: ccmode

#code language=zerolang: """
  ccmode: ccmodekind
"""

How #~"zc" will reach that compiler -- see
#> to=ccmodekind: #~"ccmodekind".


#--: target

#code language=zerolang: """
  target: String
"""

The #~"--target" triple, or the empty string for a native build.

#p: #b:"Caveat"

#~"os" and #~"arch" come from #~"--target" when one is given and from
the host otherwise. A cross #~"--cc" used #b:"without" #~"--target"
therefore leaves them describing the host while the C compiler
targets something else.

They are folded from a #b:"parse" of the triple, not from substring tests:
each #~"-" separated component is matched whole against the arm names and
their aliases, and a triple naming no operating system or two of them is
refused at the flag rather than folded. See the cross-compilation section
of #~"zc.pdoc" for the vocabulary.

#~"zl" and #~"zls" never see a #~"--target" — they resolve the triple as
empty — so a #~"require:" guard keyed on a cross target fires in
#~"zc build" and stays silent in the editor.
